read: app:doctrine-walk~2666 | Saul was consenting unto his death

=== Righteous Lot
# from 6992
--- Pitching his tent toward Sodom
kicker: Righteous Lot
lead: Righteous Lot, saved by faith while in the camp of Abram, who pitched his tent towards Sodom, representing the saved man who is taken in by the world rather than living a life of separation.
p: Worldly. Carnal. Vexed in the spirit by the filthy conversation around him, but mired in it. So much so his daughters are betrothed to men of the city. His wife, unable to leave sin behind, looks back despite the warning.
big: 2 Peter 2:7-8 | mark: just Lot, vexed with the filthy conversation of the wicked
p: As everyone and everything around us falls into wickedness, we cling to the worldly and the profane, despite knowing better. Despite having the Spirit within us.

# from 12519
--- Lusting after Egypt
kicker: Righteous Lot
p: They and the mixed multitudes among them begin to lust. To look back to Egypt as better than their miraculous salvation. To miss the life they had, and resent the heavenly bread which God provided.
p: The separation and sanctified life of the Christian can often feel like this. Working, reading scripture, praying, church... a routine that sees little variance, and if we aren't careful, can become stale and boring.
p: When I first read through Exodus and Numbers, I would be angry at the Israelites. Now, as a more mature Christian, I see how my own walk with God has likely angered Him, as I have gone after lusts of the world. Be hungry for the manna.
refs: Numbers 11:4-6; Matthew 6:11

# from 13462, 14658, 16498
--- The high places left standing
kicker: Righteous Lot
lead: A little leaven leavens the whole lump. God promises to remove the sin and temptation in our hearts, and yet we allow these temptations to remain among us.
p: The Jebusites were never fully driven out, and acted as a canker ([[Joshua 15:63]]). The kings of Judah would break down the house of Baal, but they never turn to the high places. We should seek to have a heart like David's, and strive to remove the high places in our hearts.
p: That which is holy doesn't make that which is not holy clean, and that which is unclean makes even that which is holy unclean. A little dirt in water makes all the water dirty ([[Haggai 2:12-13]]).
read: app:doctrine-walk~6992 | Righteous Lot, the backslidden believer
read: app:doctrine-walk~12519 | Lusting after Egypt and despising the manna
read: app:doctrine-walk~14658 | Remove the high places in our hearts
read: app:doctrine-walk~16498 | The unclean defiles the holy

=== The spirit of the command
# from 13348
--- Joshua's silence
kicker: The spirit of the command
p: Joshua not only conveyed the word of God, but cautioned the people to be silent, lest they feel the need to shout at another time, and in doing so disobey the word of God ([[Joshua 6:10]]).
p: This is a key point of separation. Taking not just the letter of the commandments and statutes God has provided us, but honoring the Spirit of it and not leaving ourselves open to temptation. Some professing Christians call this “legalism.” But Joshua was safeguarding them against temptation.

# from 13349, 11398, 13139
--- All things are not expedient
kicker: The spirit of the command
big: 1 Corinthians 6:12 | mark: all things are not expedient
p: We are at liberty in Christ. But not all things edify. Not all things bring a good report of the gospel to those outside the faith. Not all things serve to keep us from backsliding into sin.
p: Sobriety. Modest and gender appropriate dress. Separation from heathen practices, such as cuttings of the flesh for the dead and the marks of the pagans. These are examples of being careful in applying the spirit of the commandments of God. To be a good witness. To avoid our own temptation.
refs: Leviticus 19:28; Deuteronomy 22:5; 1 Timothy 2:9

# from 16425, 16786
--- Constant vigilance
kicker: The spirit of the command
p: The priests are instructed to change their clothes to even approach the holy chambers ([[Ezekiel 42:13-14]]). Spiritually, as a holy priesthood, we separate ourselves unto the Lord. To keep our inner chambers, our heart and mind, pure of the profane.
p: Nehemiah is gone a short time and the city of Jerusalem has descended into sin. People forget the bondage of their unregenerate life. They quickly forget chastisements. It takes constant vigilance, preaching and hard leadership to maintain even a bare minimum of standards. But it is a good work.
refs: Nehemiah 13:6-9
